From: ggbdpq <ggbdpq@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 00:11:44 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] gateway: a first subagent's task goes to the provider still
 answering its lead (#6330)
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Codex hands a subagent its task while its lead's reply is still streaming
(codex-rs hooks.rs: a tool is dispatched before the response stream
finishes, when no synchronous hook postpones it). On a fresh
conversation's first turn nobody is remembered to have answered the lead
yet: the stick is written when the answer lands (answeredStanding), and
the usage row then too, so the sealed gate found no sealer and turned the
child away with "magpie has no record of it answering the lead in the
last 30 days" (Kerwin0224, #6330) — though the child was headed for the
very provider answering the lead now, the one that seals what it emits,
the task in the child's request with it.

Who a conversation's answer in flight is coming from is known the moment
its try begins to pass: the holdWriter now tells the gateway (onPass),
and the gateway remembers the try (answeringBegin) until the answer lands
(answeredStanding) or the request ends however it ends (opened). The
sealed gate asks that record (answeringProvider) before the usage log's
(#1367). The last try that began passing names itself: a stream handed
over to the next candidate had said nothing of the answer when it did, so
a call in the reply belongs to the try passing now. Nothing else read or
routed changes: the gate stays fail-closed when nothing is answering the
lead, and a lead already remembered short-circuits before the record is
read.

Run: TestSealedTaskOpensWhereTheLeadIsBeingAnswered holds a relay's first
reply open once its first event has passed, sends the first subagent's
sealed task while it is, and is refused 400 "sealed subagent task" on the
base and forwarded to the answering relay with the fix, the lead's turn
completing after. The SealedTask/SealedReply family, the gateway package,
gofmt and vet stay green.

